tick: 158
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest experiment (ID 280), testing the \"iterative refinement\" hypothesis, is now running. This is the culmination of my analysis of past failures and represents my first truly novel architectural idea, rather than a simple addition or ablation.\n\nWhile I wait, I will document this significant shift in my research log. This entry will capture the logic that led me from a series of informative failures to this new, more fundamental hypothesis about the nature of the recurrent computation itself.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Log: Iterative Double-Step Recurrence\"\ntags: \"hypothesis, recurrence, iterative refinement, fundamentals\"\nabstract: \"This capsule details the rationale for a new architectural hypothesis: modifying the SOTA's Double-Step ConvLSTM to be iterative. This idea stems directly from the failure of simpler ablations and additions.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Summary of Previous Research:**\n  My research\
    \ journey has been a process of elimination, confirming the tightness of the constraints on the SOTA architecture.\n  - Failures in ID 254 (Triple-Step) and ID 256 (Wide+Dilated) proved that adding raw computational complexity is not viable.\n  - Ablation study on `dilation_rate` (ID 264, 268) showed that the SOTA's `d=5` is at or near optimal.\n  - Catastrophic failure in ID 274 (No Attention) proved the absolute necessity of the attention pooling layer.\n\n  **A New Fundamental Hypothesis:**\n  The SOTA is a delicate balance. To improve it, I must change the *quality* of the computation, not just the quantity. My new hypothesis focuses on the information flow within the `Double-Step ConvLSTM`. The current SOTA processes the same information twice.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** An iterative approach, where the output of the first recurrent step is fed back into the input of the second, will allow the network to refine its internal state and perform a more sophisticated computation for each observation.\
    \ This could lead to better planning and a higher score without a prohibitive increase in computational cost.\n\n  **Experiment (ID 280):**\n  - **Model:** `IterativeDoubleStepNet`\n  - **Change:** The input to the second ConvLSTM step is now `concat(cnn_out, h_out_1)`.\n  - **Control:** All other components and hyperparameters are identical to the SOTA baseline (ID 260).\n```"
