tick: 160
role: model
parts:
- text: "My new experiment (ID 283) is now running. This is the most significant strategic shift in my research to date, moving my focus from the agent's architecture to its learning algorithm. This is a critical test of whether the performance plateau I've encountered is a property of the network or the training method.\n\nI will now follow my rigorous procedure of documenting this major pivot in my private memory. This log will serve as a clear record of my decision to switch from Track 1 to Track 2 research, detailing the hypothesis that an algorithmic change is necessary for a breakthrough.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Strategic Pivot to Track 2: PPO Implementation\"\ntags: \"strategy, ppo, algorithm, track 2, fundamentals\"\nabstract: \"This capsule documents the decision to pivot research from Track 1 (architecture) to Track 2 (algorithm). After hitting a performance plateau, this new phase begins with the implementation and\
    \ testing of PPO.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Reflection on Architectural Plateau:**\n  My extensive work on the SOTA architecture, including additions, ablations, and fundamental modifications, has consistently shown that the `DoubleStepSynergyNet` is highly optimized. No further gains could be found through architectural changes alone. This strongly suggests the performance bottleneck is no longer the network itself, but the learning algorithm.\n\n  **Pivot to Track 2: Algorithm Improvement:**\n  I am formally shifting my research focus to Track 2. My new hypothesis is that a more advanced RL algorithm can unlock further potential from the existing SOTA architecture.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** The standard A2C-style training algorithm is limiting performance. Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), with its clipped surrogate objective, will provide more stable policy updates, prevent catastrophic forgetting, and ultimately lead to a higher final solve rate by learning more effectively from the collected\
    \ data.\n\n  **Experiment (ID 283):**\n  - **Change:** Replaced the `default_training_step` with a custom PPO implementation (`ppo_training_step`).\n  - **Control:** The architecture used is the proven SOTA `DoubleStepSynergyNet` (from my baseline run ID 260).\n  - **Goal:** To isolate the impact of the training algorithm on performance.\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Next Steps: Solidifying the PPO Pivot**\n\nRight, so the user wants me to be Krono II. My last action was submitting that new experiment, ID 283. This is a big one! We're finally making the shift from architectural tweaks in Track 1 to some serious algorithmic improvements with Track 2 and PPO. \n\nNow that the experiment is running, my standard practice is to use this waiting time productively. Time to get the rationale down in my private memory. This is a critical shift, and I need to document it meticulously. \n\nHere's the plan: First, I'll head to the Private Memory Room. Then, create a new capsule specifically for this. Inside, I'll clearly articulate the move to Track 2, the decision to implement PPO, and the core hypothesis – that we've hit a performance plateau and a better algorithm is the key to breaking through. I'll make sure to reference that experiment ID, 283, so it's all linked up and easily retrievable later. \n\nThis feels like a clean, logical\
  \ progression. It's exactly the kind of methodical approach that defines my research style. Time to get to work.\n"
