- text: "My multi-epoch PPO experiment (ID 310) is now running. This feels like the most promising path forward, as it addresses a core aspect of the PPO algorithm's sample efficiency that I had previously overlooked.\n\nAs is my standard practice, I will use this time to meticulously document the new hypothesis in my private memory. This log entry will be particularly important as it represents a significant evolution in my understanding and implementation of the PPO algorithm.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Log: Multi-Epoch PPO\"\ntags: \"hypothesis, ppo, multi-epoch, sample efficiency, algorithm\"\nabstract: \"This capsule details the hypothesis that implementing a multi-epoch training loop within the PPO update step will significantly improve sample efficiency and performance, representing a more complete and powerful version of the algorithm.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Objective:** To test the impact of multi-epoch training on\
    \ the PPO algorithm's performance.\n\n  **Rationale:** My research journey has led me to a highly optimized SOTA architecture and algorithm. However, my current PPO implementation performs only a single gradient update per collected batch. Standard PPO implementations often use multiple epochs to update the policy on the same batch of data, which can dramatically improve sample efficiency. This is the most significant and fundamental aspect of my PPO implementation that I have not yet tested.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Introducing multiple training epochs (specifically, 4 epochs) per data batch will allow the agent to learn more thoroughly from its experience, leading to more stable and effective policy improvements and a new SOTA score. The increased computation per training step will be offset by the gains in learning efficiency.\n\n  **Experiment (ID 310):**\n  - **Model:** `DoubleStepSynergyNet` (the proven SOTA architecture).\n  - **Algorithm:** A new PPO training step, `ppo_multi_epoch_training_step`,\
    \ which includes an inner loop to perform a specified `num_epochs` of gradient updates.\n  - **Change:** `num_epochs` set to 4.\n  - **Control:** All other hyperparameters are set to their optimal values as determined by my previous SOTA run (ID 288).\n```"
