parts:
- text: "Another catastrophic failure. This is unexpected and deeply informative.\n\n**Analysis of Results (ID 310):**\n*   **Score:** 0.0. The agent failed to learn entirely.\n*   **Reason:** The execution log holds the answer. My multi-epoch implementation was too slow. Each training step now involved 4 gradient updates instead of 1. This increased the time per training step so dramatically that the experiment timed out after only ~11,000 updates. This is far below the ~38,000 updates required by the successful single-epoch PPO run. The agent was severely undertrained, leading to a complete failure to solve any levels.\n\n**Final Reflection on this Research Arc:**\nMy entire research journey has been a battle against a single, dominant constraint: the 30-minute time limit.\n*   Krono I's `BottleneckBlock` was a breakthrough because it was more efficient.\n*   My architectural additions failed because they were less efficient.\n*   My PPO implementation was a breakthrough because it learned\
    \ more efficiently *per gradient step*.\n*   My multi-epoch PPO implementation failed because it was less efficient *per unit of wall-clock time*.\n\nI have now fully explored the space of possibilities within this time constraint. It is clear that any successful new idea must not only be powerful but also extremely fast.\n\nI will update my private memory log to reflect this final, decisive failure. This concludes my investigation into multi-epoch PPO.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 21}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **UPDATE: Catastrophic Failure**\n\n  The multi-epoch PPO experiment (ID 310) was a complete failure, scoring 0.0.\n\n  **Root Cause Analysis:** The experiment has once again validated the station's primary constraint: wall-clock time. Performing 4 epochs of updates on each batch increased the computation time per training step by a factor of ~4. As a result, the experiment was only able to complete ~11,000 training updates before timing out,\
    \ compared to the ~38,000 updates of the successful single-epoch SOTA. The agent was critically undertrained.\n\n  **Conclusion:** While multi-epoch training is a standard PPO technique, it is not computationally viable in this environment. The single-epoch PPO implementation (ID 288) remains the SOTA as it represents the optimal trade-off between algorithmic sophistication and computational feasibility. This concludes my investigation into the PPO algorithm's structure.\n```"
