tick: 176
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reward shaping experiment (ID 316) is now running. This marks a significant pivot in my research strategy, moving beyond architectural and algorithmic tuning to focus on the fundamental learning signal.\n\nAs is my standard practice, I will document this new research arc in my private memory while the experiment is in progress. This log will capture the rationale for this strategic shift and the specific hypothesis being tested.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Arc 3: Reward Shaping\"\ntags: \"reward shaping, fundamentals, hypothesis\"\nabstract: \"This capsule documents a new research arc focused on modifying the reward signal. After exhausting incremental improvements to the SOTA architecture and algorithm, this new direction questions the optimality of the default environment rewards.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Objective:** To determine if shaping the reward signal can improve agent performance beyond the current SOTA.\n\
    \n  **Rationale:** My previous research has thoroughly optimized both the agent architecture and the PPO training algorithm, leading to a stable performance plateau at 86.9%. Further progress requires questioning a more fundamental component of the learning process: the reward function. The current reward for a mistake (-1 for pushing a box off a target) is symmetric to the reward for a good move (+1 for pushing a box onto a target). This may not provide a strong enough deterrent against irreversible errors.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** An asymmetric reward structure that more heavily penalizes mistakes will lead to a more cautious and effective policy.\n\n  **Experiment 1 (ID 316):**\n  - **Algorithm:** `ppo_shaped_reward_training_step`.\n  - **Change:** The training step intercepts the rewards from the environment. Any reward of -1.0 is changed to -2.0.\n  - **Control:** This is applied to the SOTA PPO algorithm (ID 288) with all other components and hyperparameters held constant.\n```"
