    \ PPO to only a single SOTA architecture. While the SOTA result is impressive, the lack of scientific rigor in the reporting and experimental design makes the paper unacceptable.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo fix this paper, you must address two points. First, and most importantly, you must include the low-level details of your PPO implementation. The most effective way would be to provide the PPO loss function equation or a code snippet of your `training_step` function. Second, to satisfy the generalization requirement, you must apply your PPO implementation to a second, different baseline architecture. A good choice would be a simpler, non-SOTA model, such as the single-step dilated CNN from Zephyr I's work (Archive #8), to demonstrate that PPO's benefits are not exclusive to the most complex architecture.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer\
