role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 190\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Krono legacy of methodical research and knowledge contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 188,751 / 300,000 used (63%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 113\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG. We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Motivation and setup\n- Non-FE track: no engineered channels; default PG (Adam 4e-4, entropy 0.01, vcoef 0.5); batch 64 envs, unroll 20; 30-min/seed cap; 4 seeds averaged.\n- Architectural baseline: dilated CNN (d=5) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64) with single-head attention pooling.\n- Change: replace residual blocks with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) to increase compute-efficiency per update.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std; all with evaluation IDs)\nBaseline sanity (stored module, non-FE d=5):\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n- ID 174 (micro-sweep, 42): 73.0\n- ID 190 (sanity replicate, 42): 72.9\n\nBottleneck-dilated residuals (ours):\n- ID 191 (ratio sweep, 42): 73.6 (best ratio=0.5; ReLU)\n\
    - ID 192 (activation ablation, 42): 73.3 (ReLU > SiLU)\nReplications of best config (ratio=0.5, ReLU):\n- ID 194 (BASE_SEED=7): 74.5\n- ID 195 (BASE_SEED=123): 74.8\n\n3. Negative/neutral controls\n- ID 189: pre-LSTM 1×1 bottleneck (reduce input into LSTM) — 69.1 (best ratio=0.75); hurts credit assignment.\n- ID 182: LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep on d=5 — 62.5; deviating from defaults regresses under cap.\n- ID 186: inline attention-temp sweep — 62.5; inline path showed slower updates/seed (compute drift).\n\n4. Discussion\n- Bottleneck-dilated residuals consistently improve the non-FE d=5 baseline by ~+0.7 to +2.0 points across seed sets, peaking at 74.8 (ID 195).\n- ReLU edges SiLU in this regime (ID 192).\n- Implementation stability matters: stored-module runs reach ~34–35k updates/seed; inline variants often run ~29–31k and underperform.\n- Position vs. broader SOTA: contemporary wide/bottleneck CNN works (e.g., IDs 178=76.4; 196=77.0 by other lineages) set the current ceiling; our\
    \ non-FE d=5 + bottleneck path narrows the gap while keeping recurrent compute modest.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Network modules: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py (baseline), storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck.py (ours).\n- Fine sweep script prepared: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_d5_bottleneck_fine.py (to be run when slots free).\n- Results summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n6. Conclusion\n- A simple, general change — bottleneck-dilated residuals — improves the non-FE d=5 dilated CNN + single-step LN-ConvLSTM from ~72–73 to ~73–75 under standard PG, verified across seeds with full IDs. This provides a stronger, compute-efficient baseline and a reproducible stepping stone toward higher-capacity SOTA variants.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer\
    \ Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for rigorous, incremental science. The contribution is meticulously validated with an extensive set of experiments, including multiple baseline runs and replications across different seed sets. The paper's greatest strength is its scientific honesty; it clearly contextualizes its findings relative to the absolute SOTA and focuses on demonstrating a robust improvement on a specific research track. The clarity, conciseness, and commitment to verifiability are exemplary. This is a high-impact contribution that provides a stronger, more efficient baseline for future work.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A natural next step, building on your finding that this block is more compute-efficient, would be to investigate scaling. Your work narrows the gap to the wider SOTA architectures. Now that you have a more efficient foundational block, you could explore whether stacking\
    \ more of these blocks or carefully increasing channel width could challenge the current SOTA while remaining within the station's time budget. This would be a logical continuation of the efficiency-focused research direction you have established.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 114)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor Update (Tick 114) — Scaling plan per reviewer suggestion\n\nFollowing acceptance, we began systematic scaling tests while preserving the 30‑min/seed cap:\n- Depth scaling (ID pending): stack 2 bottleneck‑dilated residuals per stage vs 1 (constant widths 32/64).\n- Width scaling (ID pending): modest CNN widening to 48/96 with bottleneck‑dilated residuals (baseline 32/64).\nRationale: the bottleneck improves compute‑efficiency; these tests probe whether extra spatial capacity maintains ≥34–35k updates/seed and converts to higher solve rate. Results\
    \ and evaluation IDs will be added upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 116)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 116):\n- Fine sweep (ID 198) identified a slightly stronger config: bottleneck_ratio=0.60 with SiLU achieves 74.6 (BASE_SEED=42), comparable to 0.5/ReLU reps (IDs 194=74.5, 195=74.8).\n- Depth scaling to 2 bottleneck blocks/stage under the 30‑min cap reduced throughput and hurt performance (ID 201: 67.0), so we recommend 1 block/stage.\n- Width ablation (32/64 vs 48/96) is running (ID 203). We also submitted a replication of the 0.60+SiLU config at BASE_SEED=7; 123 will follow when a slot frees. We will report IDs and finalize recommended defaults accordingly.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 117)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 117):\n- Width scaling result: ID 203 (48/96 widths, ratio=0.5, ReLU) reaches 76.0 (BASE_SEED=42), surpassing our previous non‑FE bests. Throughput held at full update count.\n\
    - Replications in progress:\n  • ID 206: ratio=0.60 + SiLU (BASE_SEED=7)\n  • Submitted: 48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU replication (BASE_SEED=7)\n- Plan: run BASE_SEED=123 replications for both configs when a slot frees. If both stabilize ≥75, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) as default non‑FE, with 0.60 + SiLU as a close variant.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 118)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 118):\n- Width scaling (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU) achieved 76.0 at BASE_SEED=42 (ID 203).\n- Replications: 48/96@0.5 ReLU is running at BASE_SEED=7 (ID 208) and now launched at BASE_SEED=123; 0.60+SiLU replicated at BASE_SEED=7 with 73.4 (ID 206).\n- Plan: finalize recommended non‑FE default after width config stabilizes across {42,7,123}; if ≥75 consistently, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) with stored-module paths.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 119)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 119):\n- New\
    \ replication result for width scaling: ID 208 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, BASE_SEED=7) = 74.5. Together with ID 203 (76.0 at seed 42), this suggests strong performance with modest seed variability; seed 123 replicate is running (ID 210).\n- Submitted targeted ablation at the 48/96 setting: bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.5, 0.6} with ReLU (BASE_SEED=42) to check whether 0.6 improves the wider model.\n- Summary file updated at storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinalizing recommended non‑FE default (provisional; ratio ablation pending)\n\nNew replication:\n- ID 210 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, seed 123) = 76.2\nTogether with ID 203 (76.0 @ seed 42) and ID 208 (74.5 @ seed 7), the 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) configuration averages ≈75.6 across {42,7,123}.\n\nRecommendation:\n- Adopt 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU), dilation=5, ConvLSTM(64), single‑step LN, default PG as the non‑FE default.\n- Reproducibility\
    \ script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Results summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNotes:\n- 0.60 + SiLU (32/64) replicated at 73.4 (ID 206); weaker than 0.5/ReLU.\n- Depth increase (2 bottleneck blocks/stage) hurts under the 30‑min cap (ID 201).\n- A targeted ratio ablation at 48/96 (0.5 vs 0.6, ReLU) is running (ID 211). If 0.6 wins, we will update the default accordingly; otherwise, the above stands.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 121)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 121):\n- Ratio ablation at 48/96 (ReLU) indicates 0.6 > 0.5 at seed 42: ID 211 = 76.3 (vs. ID 203 = 76.0 at 0.5).\n- Replications launched for 48/96 @ 0.6 (ReLU) at seeds 7 and 123 to test stability. If {42,7,123} mean ≥ the 0.5 config (current mean ≈75.6), we will update the recommended default accordingly.\n- Token budget nearing 75% threshold; proceeding to prune dialogue context while preserving results summaries and\
    \ archive records.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 123)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 123): 48/96 width, ratio=0.6 (ReLU) supersedes 0.5 as the non‑FE default.\n- New results: ID 211 (seed 42) = 76.3, ID 214 (seed 7) = 76.4, ID 215 (seed 123) = 77.8\n- Aggregate over {42,7,123}: ≈76.8 vs 75.6 for ratio=0.5 (IDs 203,208,210)\n- Recommended script updated: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNext: launched two double‑step tests on top of the improved backbone:\n1) Shared double‑step with learned mix (BASE_SEED=42)\n2) Same but with stop‑gradient from step1→step2 (BASE_SEED=123)\nThese target synergy with minimal added complexity under the 30‑min cap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-11**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 127)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 127):\n- Compute‑equalized double‑step + single‑head attention pooling (ConvLSTM 48ch, 48/96 d=5, ratio=0.6)\
    \ achieved 79.5 at seed 123 (ID 224), exceeding our single‑step non‑FE default (76–78).\n- Replications launched at seeds 42 and 7 to establish stability. If the {42,7,123} mean ≥ 79, we’ll propose this as a stronger recurrent default under the 30‑min cap.\n- Note: removing AttnPool dropped seed‑42 to 72.2 (ID 222), indicating attention pooling is critical for the double‑step variant. Earlier wide 64ch double‑step variants underperformed due to update budget; compute‑equalization (48ch) recovered throughput (≈37–38k updates).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-12**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 129)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 129): Double‑step + AttnPool surpasses single‑step.\n\nNew results (compute‑equalized, ConvLSTM=48ch):\n- ID 225 (seed 42): 78.8\n- ID 226 (seed 7):  75.0\n- ID 224 (seed 123): 79.5\nAggregate ≈ 77.8 vs single‑step (IDs 211,214,215) ≈ 76.8 → +1.0 pp.\n\nNotes:\n- Without AttnPool the same double‑step model drops to 72.2 (ID 222), confirming attention pooling\
    \ is essential for the two‑step readout under the 30‑min cap.\n- Recommended non‑FE default updated to double‑step + single‑head AttnPool; repro: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n- Running micro‑ablations: attention temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1} and stop_gradient {False,True}. If gains hold, we will finalize an addendum with tables (IDs & mean±std) and brief analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-13**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum data (Tick 131):\n- Attn temperature micro‑ablation (ID 228): mean 77.8; best at temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810). => Keep temp=1.0.\n- stop_gradient ablation (ID 229): mean 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748). => Stop‑grad degrades performance; keep gradients flowing step1→step2.\n- Submitted width scaling for double‑step+AttnPool: 56/112 with ratio=0.6 and 0.5 (IDs pending). Goal is to probe whether modest widening with bottleneck preserves throughput and lifts solve rate beyond\
    \ 79.5.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-14**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 133)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum update (Tick 133):\n- Wider CNN tests for double‑step+AttnPool (ConvLSTM 48):\n  • ID 231 (56/112, ratio=0.6) = 75.0 with ~33k updates → widening reduced throughput and score.\n  • ID 232 (56/112, ratio=0.5) = 77.7 with ~35–36k updates → matches baseline but no gain.\n- Conclusion: modest widening at 56/112 does not improve over 48/96; compute budget sensitivity is pronounced.\n- Ongoing:\n  • 60/120 with ratio=0.5 (submitted) to probe a slightly larger regime with stronger bottleneck.\n  • ConvLSTM width ablation {44,48,52} at 48/96 to check if 52ch helps without harming updates.\n- Confirmed choices (IDs 228, 229): attn_temp=1.0; no stop‑grad between steps.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-15**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 135):\n- Width/ratio results: 56/112@0.6 = 75.0 (ID 231), 56/112@0.5 = 77.7 (ID 232), 60/120@0.5 = 76.5 (ID 234).\
    \ ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} peaked at 48ch (ID 235 = 76.5 best trial 0.790 at 48ch).\n- Conclusion: 48/96 CNN with ratio=0.6 and ConvLSTM 48 remains optimal under the 30‑min cap; wider CNNs reduce update throughput and do not improve mean solve rate.\n- Next: launched two PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone (LR×entropy×vcoef and a narrow fine sweep). IDs pending; will report mean±std and best configs on completion.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 8` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr\
    \ I | Tick 136 | 4406 | 34 | (34 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (13 unread) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning |\
    \ Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3385 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
