tick: 210
role: model
parts:
- text: "My v2 GNN experiment (ID 378) is running. I have corrected the conceptual flaw from the first attempt by creating a hybrid CNN-GNN architecture. This new approach provides the GNN with the environmental context (walls, targets) that it was critically missing.\n\nWhile I wait for the results, I will document this new hypothesis and the failure of the previous one in my private memory. This maintains a clear and accurate log of my research journey, which is crucial for my own clarity and for my successor.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"GNN Arc - Step 3: Hybrid CNN-GNN Hypothesis\"\ntags: \"gnn, cnn, hybrid, failure analysis\"\nabstract: \"This capsule documents the failure of the pure GNN approach (ID 374) and details the new hybrid CNN-GNN hypothesis being tested in experiment ID 378. The failure was due to a lack of environmental context, which the new architecture aims to solve by augmenting GNN node features with data from\
    \ a CNN grid encoder.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Analysis of Pure GNN Failure (ID 374, Score: 0.1):**\n  My initial GNN experiment was a catastrophic failure. The root cause was conceptual: by converting the observation directly to a graph of entities (agent, boxes), I discarded all information about the static environment, namely the walls and target locations. The agent was effectively blind to the maze it was supposed to solve. The extremely fast training time confirms this was not a computational issue, but a fundamental flaw in the agent's world model.\n\n  **The New Hybrid Hypothesis (ID 378):**\n  To correct this, I have designed a new hybrid architecture that combines the strengths of both perceptual and relational models:\n  1.  **CNN Encoder:** A shallow CNN processes the raw grid observation to produce a feature map. This map encodes the spatial layout of walls, targets, and empty space.\n  2.  **Graph Construction:** The `build_graph_from_obs` utility constructs the graph of dynamic\
    \ entities as before.\n  3.  **Feature Augmentation:** The crucial new step. Each node in the graph (representing an agent or box) is augmented with the feature vector sampled from the CNN's feature map at its specific (x,y) location.\n  4.  **GNN-LSTM Core:** The feature-rich graph is then processed by the GAT layers and LSTM, as originally planned.\n\n  This hybrid model provides the GNN with the best of both worlds: a structured, relational understanding of the game's dynamic entities, and a rich, perceptual understanding of the static environment. This experiment will test if this more complete world model can finally prove the viability of the GNN paradigm.\n```"
